# Countries, Capitals and Currencies: The Static GK Backbone

> No section of general knowledge returns more marks per minute of
> study than the countries table. The trick is that exams rarely ask
> the famous pair — they ask the *second* city, the *unexpected*
> capital, the currency nobody can spell. Canberra, not Sydney.
> Ankara, not Istanbul. Bern, not Zurich. This lesson drills the
> exam-famous rows and the reasons behind them.

## 1. The Unexpected Capitals

[definition]
A **decoy capital** is a city more famous than the actual capital —
Sydney, Istanbul, Toronto, Mumbai — which the exam offers as a trap
option. Learning the decoys as a set is faster than learning the
capitals one by one.
[/definition]

| Country | Capital | The decoy (wrong option) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Australia | Canberra | Sydney, Melbourne |
| Turkey | Ankara | Istanbul |
| Canada | Ottawa | Toronto |
| Brazil | Brasilia | Rio de Janeiro, Sao Paulo |
| USA | Washington, D.C. | New York |
| New Zealand | Wellington | Auckland |
| Switzerland | Bern (de facto) | Zurich, Geneva |
| Pakistan | Islamabad | Karachi, Lahore |
| Myanmar | Nay Pyi Taw | Yangon |
| Kazakhstan | Astana | Almaty |
| Nigeria | Abuja | Lagos |
| Brazil's neighbour Bolivia | La Paz (seat of govt) / Sucre (constitutional) | — |
| South Africa | Pretoria (executive), Cape Town (legislative), Bloemfontein (judicial) | Johannesburg |
| Sri Lanka | Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte | Colombo (commercial capital) |
| Netherlands | Amsterdam (constitutional) | The Hague (seat of government) |
| Bhutan | Thimphu | — |

South Africa is the classic three-capital case, and the option list
usually offers only Pretoria and Johannesburg — pick Pretoria for
"administrative/executive capital." Sri Lanka's official capital is
the legislative seat **Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte**, with Colombo as
the commercial hub; exams phrase the question as "administrative
capital" to make Kotte the answer.

## 2. Currencies with Exotic Names

| Country | Currency | Country | Currency |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Japan | Yen | South Korea | Won |
| China | Renminbi (yuan) | North Korea | Won |
| UK | Pound sterling | USA | Dollar |
| Russia | Rouble | European Union | Euro |
| Bhutan | Ngultrum | Maldives | Rufiyaa |
| Nepal | Nepalese rupee | Sri Lanka | Sri Lankan rupee |
| Bangladesh | Taka | Pakistan | Pakistani rupee |
| Afghanistan | Afghani | Myanmar | Kyat |
| Thailand | Baht | Indonesia | Rupiah |
| Malaysia | Ringgit | Singapore | Singapore dollar |
| UAE | Dirham | Saudi Arabia | Riyal |
| Iran | Rial | Israel | New shekel |
| Turkey | Lira | Egypt | Egyptian pound |
| Nigeria | Naira | Kenya | Shilling |
| Ethiopia | Birr | Ghana | Cedi |
| South Africa | Rand | Russia's neighbours... | — |
| Sweden | Krona | Norway | Krone |
| Denmark | Krone | Iceland | Krona |
| Switzerland | Swiss franc | Poland | Zloty |
| Brazil | Real | Argentina | Argentine peso |
| Mongolia | Tugrik | Vietnam | Dong |
| Cambodia | Riel | Laos | Kip |

The Scandinavian trap: Sweden uses the **krona**, Norway and Denmark
the **krone** — the difference is one letter, and papers time their
question to catch exactly that slip. The Gulf pair runs dirham (UAE)
against riyal (Saudi Arabia), with Qatar's **riyal** and Oman's
**rial** adding further confusion at the margins.

## 3. The Subcontinental Set and the Neighbourhood

India's neighbours deserve a dedicated pass because their rows are
the most frequently asked and the most frequently confused:

| Country | Capital | Currency |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Afghanistan | Kabul | Afghani |
| Bangladesh | Dhaka | Taka |
| Bhutan | Thimphu | Ngultrum |
| China | Beijing | Renminbi (yuan) |
| Myanmar | Nay Pyi Taw | Kyat |
| Nepal | Kathmandu | Nepalese rupee |
| Pakistan | Islamabad | Pakistani rupee |
| Sri Lanka | Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte | Sri Lankan rupee |
| Maldives | Male | Rufiyaa |

The Bhutan–Maldives pair (Ngultrum and Rufiyaa) is the highest-yield
row in the table: both currencies are unique to GK papers, both
countries are small, and both questions are near-guaranteed in
railway and SSC papers. The **Ngultrum is pegged to the Indian
rupee**, a favourite secondary fact.

## 4. Regions, Groups, and Flags Worth Knowing

Some currency questions arrive dressed as geography or grouping
questions. The eurozone's members use the euro, but **not the UK,
Switzerland, Sweden, Denmark, or Norway** — a list papers love to
invert. The Gulf Cooperation Council states (Saudi, UAE, Qatar,
Bahrain, Kuwait, Oman) do **not** share one currency despite the
dirham–riyal family resemblance.

The dollar zone beyond the USA — Singapore, Canada, Australia, New
Zealand, and Hong Kong (HKD) — each adds a country adjective to the
dollar, and a paper asks "which country's currency is the loonie?"
(the Canadian dollar) as a finisher. Finally, one last oddball fact
for tie-breakers: the world's oldest still-used currency unit is
usually cited as the **British pound sterling**, a claim examiners
occasionally deploy as a standalone question.

[example]
A railway paper asks: "The currency of Bhutan is:" with options
Ngultrum, Kyat, Taka, Rufiyaa. All four are south/south-east Asian
currencies, so the region knowledge does not help — only the row
does: Ngultrum (Bhutan), Kyat (Myanmar), Taka (Bangladesh), Rufiyaa
(Maldives). The follow-up asks Myanmar's capital with Yangon as the
trap; the answer is Nay Pyi Taw. Decoys cluster in the same
neighbourhood, so study the neighbourhood as a block.
[/example]

[summary]
Master the decoy capitals first — Canberra, Ankara, Ottawa,
Brasilia, Washington D.C., Wellington, Bern, Islamabad, Nay Pyi
Taw, Abuja — because that is where marks are lost. Then learn the
exotic currency column: Ngultrum (Bhutan), Rufiyaa (Maldives), Taka
(Bangladesh), Afghani, Kyat, Baht, Rupiah, Ringgit, Dirham, Riyal,
Shekel, Naira, Shilling, Rand, Real, Won, Krona/Krone distinction.
South Africa's three capitals, Sri Lanka's Kotte, and the
eurozone's absentees (UK, Switzerland, Scandinavia) are the classic
finisher facts.
[/summary]
